{# The card, drawn as the card.

   It mirrors what is typed rather than owning it — the inputs are the state,
   this is a reading of them. Two faces: the front carries the number, name and
   expiry; the back carries the security code where the card carries it, so
   focusing the CVV field turns the card over and shows you where to look
   instead of explaining it in a tooltip.

   The scheme logo is whichever one the number identifies, not a row of four.
   A wrong-looking logo is a faster correction than a validation message. #}
<div class="pay-card js-pay-card" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="pay-card__faces">
        <div class="pay-card__face pay-card__face--front">
            <div class="pay-card__top">
                <span class="pay-card__brand js-card-brand">Card</span>
                <span class="pay-card__wifi"><i class="fas fa-wifi"></i></span>
            </div>

            <div class="pay-card__chip"></div>

            <div class="pay-card__number js-card-number">•••• •••• •••• ••••</div>

            <div class="pay-card__bottom">
                <div class="pay-card__field">
                    <span class="pay-card__label">Card holder</span>
                    <span class="pay-card__value js-card-name">YOUR NAME</span>
                </div>
                <div class="pay-card__field pay-card__field--expiry">
                    <span class="pay-card__label">Expires</span>
                    <span class="pay-card__value js-card-expiry">MM / YY</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="pay-card__face pay-card__face--back">
            <div class="pay-card__stripe"></div>
            <div class="pay-card__signature">
                <span class="pay-card__cvv js-card-cvv">•••</span>
            </div>
            <p class="pay-card__back-note">The security code is printed here, on the back of your card.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
